Members of the Welsh Assembly ensure that they are "similar to Mupetkom"
Some members of the assembly are appalled by the proposition to now appoint MWP (Member of the Welsh Parliament).
This was due to plans to change the name of the assembly to the Welsh Parliament.
Members of the Assembly in the entire political spectrum are concerned that they will be ridiculed.
A member of the Labour Assembly said they were worried in their group “because the name rhymes with Twp and Pwp.”
For readers outside of Wales: in Welsh, twp means silly and pwp poops.
Plaid Cymru, a member of the Plaid Cymru party, said that their group as a whole was “unsatisfied,” and suggested other options.
The Welsh Conservative said that his group was “open-minded” about name change, but at the same time pointed out that there is a small difference in pronunciation between MWP and Muppet.
In this context, the pronunciation of the Welsh letter "w" is similar to the pronunciation of the letter "u" in the Yorkshire dialect.
The commission of the assembly, which is currently preparing the draft legislation for the presentation of changes to the name, stated: "The final decision on possible descriptors for the members of the assembly will, of course, have to be taken by the members themselves."
The Welsh Assembliament, by the Government of Wales Act 2017, was given the power to change its name.
In June, the Commission published the results of a public consultation on the proposals and received broad support for the appointment of the Welsh Parliament Assembly.
For the members of the assembly, the Commission chose the name "Welsh Member Parliament" or "WMP", but in the public consultation the "MWP" option received the most support.
Members of the assembly are apparently proposing alternatives, but reaching a consensus will be a difficult task for Elin Jones, the President-in-Office, who must submit draft legislation on amendments within a few weeks.
Legislation on reforms will also include other changes to the functioning of the Assembly, including rules on the incompatibility of the performance of members of the Assembly and the formation of a system of committees.
The members of the Assembly will participate in the final vote on their naming in the debate on the legislation.
The Macedonians will decide with a referendum on changing the name of the state
Voters will vote on Sunday to change their country's name to "Republic of North Macedonia".
With a vote, they want to resolve a decades-long conflict with neighboring Greece, which has its own province called Macedonia.
For a long time, Athens insisted that the name of their northern neighbour represented the right to their territory and repeatedly opposed its application for membership of the EU and NATO.
The Macedonian President Gjorge Ivanov, an opponent of the plebiscite on the name change, said he would disregard the vote.
However, supporters of the referendum, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that changing the name is just the price they have to pay to join the EU and NATO.
Bells in St. Martin’s Church Were Silenced Because Harlem Churches Afraid Troubles
