At churches, however, he is worried that the money from the sale of funds will not last as long as the leaders expect, and that they will soon be at the outset again.
He added that the churches could be replaced by residential buildings with a common administration (condominium), where there will be people who won’t help shelters in the neighborhood left.
“The vast majority of people who buy condominiums in these buildings will be white,” he said, “and that’s why it will quickly come the day when all these churches will be closed, because it’s unlikely that people who move to these condominiums will become members of these churches.”
Both churches were built by white religious communities before Harlem became a black metropolis – Metropolitan Community in 1870 and St. Martin’s a decade later.
The original White Methodist ecclesiastical community emigrated in the 1930s.
The building was taken over by the black religious community, which performed the liturgy nearby.
St. Martin’s was taken over by a black religious community led by venerable John Howard Johnson, who, on 125 Street, Harlemo’s main shopping street, led a boycott of vendors who didn’t want to employ or support blacks.
The 1939 fire severely damaged the building, but when the parishers of Father Johnson planned to rebuild, they commissioned bells to kiss.
Venerable David Johnson, the son of his father Johnson and his successor at St. Martin’s Church, proudly called the bells “bells for poor people.”
An expert who knocked in July named them a little differently: “cultural treasure” and an “irreplaceable historical instrument”.
Tiffany Ng from the University of Michigan also pointed out that these were the first knocking bells played by a black musician, Dionisio A. Link, who went 18 years ago to Riverside Church, where we knock on bigger bells.
Mr Merriweather said he had not been replaced in St. Martin’s Church.
In the church of St. Martin’s in the last few months, an intricate story with architects and contractors unfolds; some were brought by ecclesiastical laity and others by a diocese.
Sacristy – the governing body of the parish, composed of laity – wrote to the diocese in July that it was concerned that the diocese would “try to transfer costs” to the parish, although the parish was not included in the employment of architects and contractors sent by the diocese.
Some parishioners complained about transparency by the diocese.
Shark injured 13-year-old while catching lobsters in California
Officials say a shark was attacked and injured by a 13-year-old dog in California on Saturday while lobster hunting at the opening of the lobster hunting season.
The attack took place just before 7 a.m. near Beacon’s Beach in Encinitas.
Chad Hammel told KSWB-TV television in San Diego that he was diving with his friends for about half an hour on Saturday morning when he heard the boy screaming for help, then swimming with the group to him and helping him pull out of the water.
Hammel said that at first he thought that the boy was just thrilled because he caught a lobster, and then he realized he was screaming “He bitten me!
“He bit me!”
When he got to the boy, Hammel noticed that he had a torn entire collarbone.
"I yelled to everyone to get out of the water: 'There's a shark in the water!'" he added.
The boy was transported by helicopter to the Rady Children's Hospital in San Diego, where he remains in critical condition.
The type of shark responsible for the attack is unknown.
Larry Giles said in a press release that a shark had been spotted in the area a few weeks earlier, but it was found that it was not a dangerous species.
Giles added that the victim suffered severe injuries to the upper torso.
Responsibles for 48 hours blocked access from Ponto Beach in Casablad to Swami’s Beach in Ecinitas for investigation and safety.
Giles said there are more than 135 species of sharks in the area, but most are not considered dangerous.
Sainsbury’s plans to enter British beauty market
Sainsbury’s comes to Boots, Superdrugs and Debenhams with beautiful corners as they have in department stores and with professional staff.
As part of its entry into Britain's £2.8 billion beauty market and is still growing, while sales of clothing and household appliances are declining, larger beauty corners will be tested in 11 stores across the country, and in more stores next year if they prove successful.
They decided to invest in beauty products, because supermarkets are looking for ways to fill the shelves that were previously used for TVs, microwaves and household appliances.
Sainsbury’s says they will double the range of their beauty products to 3000 products and include brands such as Revlon, Essie, Tweezerman and Dr. PawPaw for the first time.
L’Oreal, Maybelline and Burt’s Bees will also get more space in areas with brands similar to those in stores such as Boots.
Supermarket also revives its boutique range of cosmetics, so most products are vegan – something that younger shoppers increasingly demand.
In addition, Fragrance Shop’s perfume store will test the concession in two Sainsbury’s stores. The first opened last week in Croydon, South London, and the second will open in Selly Oak, Birmingham until the end of the year.
Due to online shopping and daily shopping for smaller amounts of food in local stores, supermarkets need to work harder to persuade people to visit.
Mike Coupe, CEO of Sainsbury’s, said retail stores would be similar to supermarkets as a chain of supermarkets tries to fight the discontomers of Aldi and Lidl with several services and non-food products.
Sainsbury’s opened Argos outlets in hundreds of stores, as well as Habitat stores since it bought both chains two years ago, increasing grocery sales and making acquisitions more profitable.
Previous attempts by the supermarket to overhaul their departments with cosmetics and pharmaceuticals ended unsuccessfully.
Sainsbury’s embarked on a joint feat with the Boots chain at the beginning of the 21 st century, but the alliance ended after a dispute over distributing drugstore profits in their supermarkets.
The new strategy comes after Sainsbury’s sold its pharmaceutical company for GBP 125 million three years ago with 281 stores to Celesio, which owns the Lloyds Pharmacy chain.
Lloyds is expected to play a role in this plan to expand the range of luxury skincare brands in four stores, including La Roche-Posay and Vichy brands.
Paul Mills-Hicks, commercial director at Sainsbury’s, said: “We’ve changed the looks and impressions of our beautie corners to improve the environment for our customers.
We have also invested in specially trained associates who will be available for counselling.
Our range of brands is designed to meet all needs, and an attractive environment and convenient locations mean that we are now an interesting beauty destination that is a challenge for the old way of shopping.”
