Killing Pig Murmured Chinese Farmer to Death
According to local media reports, at a market in the southwest of China, a pig attacked and killed a farmer.
A man who was identified only with the surname "Yuan" was found dead with a sliced artery and covered with blood near a pork at a market in Liupanshui, Guizhou province.
A pig breeder prepares to vaccinate pigs in a pig farm on May 30, 2005 in Xining, Qinghai Province, China.
It is reported that she and her cousin came from neighboring Yunnan province on Wednesday to sell 15 pigs at the market.
The next morning his cousin found him dead, and the doors of the neighboring pigsty were opened.
He said there was a big bachelor of a pig with a bloody muzzle in the pigsty.
Forensic investigation has confirmed that a 250-kilogram pig killed a farmer to death.
“Branzer’s legs were bloody and disgusted,” the “Wu” was quoted at Guiyang Evening News.
Recordings of the security camera showed that Yuang came to the market on Thursday at 4:40 a.m. in order to feed the pigs.
His body was found about an hour later.
The animal that attacked Yuana was neither his nor his cousin.
The market manager told Evening News that the pig had been imprisoned so he wouldn't invade anyone else while the police were gathering evidence at the scene.
Yuan's family and market management are expected to negotiate compensation for his death.
Although infrequently, cases of attacks of pigs on humans have already been reported.
In 2016, a pig attacked a woman and her husband on their Massachusetts farm, and the man suffered serious bodily injuries.
Ten years earlier, a 300-kilogram pig squeezed a Welsh peasant against a tractor, but then his wife chased away the animal.
