Hurricane Rosa threatens Pheonix, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City with Sudden Floods (which may be useful for arid areas)
Tropical depression rarely reaches Arizona, but that’s exactly what’s probably going to happen at the beginning of next week, as Rosa’s remaining hurricane energy paves the way through the southwest of the desert and carries the risk of sudden flooding.
The National Weather Service has already issued warnings against sudden flooding for Monday and Tuesday for Western Arizona, southern and eastern Nevada, southeastern California and Utah, including Phoenix, Flagstaff, Las Vegas and Salt Lake City.
Hurricane Rosa is expected to rampage over Phoenix on Tuesday and will be approaching late Monday with rain.
The Phoenix National Weather Service tweeted that “ten tropical cyclones alone have retained the status of a tropical storm or depression within 300 kilometers from Phoenix since 1950!
Hurricane Katrina (1967) raged more than 60 kilometers from Arizona.”
The most recent models of the National Hurricane Center envisage 50 to 100 mm of precipitation with individual precipitation up to 150 mm at Mogollon Rome in Arizona.
In other areas of the southwestern desert, including Rockies and Great Basin areas, 25 to 50 mm of precipitation is expected, individual precipitation up to 100 mm is possible.
For areas beyond the risk of sudden flooding, Hurricane Rosa rain may be a real blessing, as the region has been affected by drought.
Although flooding is a very serious problem, part of the precipitation is likely to be beneficial, as there is currently a dry period in the southwest.
“According to the U.S. Sushi Tracking Center, over 40 percent of Arizona is experiencing the least extreme drought.”
Hurricane Rosa’s path first leads to the mainland of the Mexican peninsula Baja California.
Rosa, who still has hurricane power on Sunday morning with a maximum wind speed of 137 kilometers per hour, is 620 kilometers south of Punte Eugenie in Mexico and moves north at a speed of 19 kilometers per hour.
The storm comes into contact with the cooler waters in the Pacific and therefore weakens.
It is expected to reach the mainland in Mexico with the strength of a tropical thunderstorm on a Monday afternoon or evening.
Precipitation in parts of Mexico can be abundant and therefore pose a high risk of flooding.
"From the Baja California peninsula to the north-west Sonora we expect 75 to 150 mm of precipitation with the possibility of 250 mm precipitation," weather.com reported.
Hurricane Rosa will then move north across Mexico like a tropical storm before reaching Arizone in the early hours of the early hours of Tuesday as a tropical depression that will then travel through Arizona and towards southern Utah until late night on Tuesday.
“The greatest danger we expect from Hurricane Ros or its remains is the heavy rains on the Baja Califonia peninsula in northwest Sonora and in the American southwestern desert,” the National Hurricane Center said.
These precipitation can cause life-threatening sudden floods and debris in deserts and landslides in mountain areas.
