Hundreds killed in Indonesian earthquake and tsunami, death toll rises
The authorities reported on Saturday that at least 384 people had died in an earthquake and tsunamium that struck the Indonesian island of Sulawesi, many being swept away by giant waves banging against the coast.
Hundreds of people gathered on Friday at a beach festival in Palu, when six-meter waves hit the coast at dusk and took many to their deaths, destroying everything along their way.
The tsunami was followed by a 7.5-degree earthquake.
“When a tsunami threat arose yesterday, people stayed on the beach and didn’t immediately run away, so they became victims,” Sutopo Purwo Nugroho, an Indonesian BNPB accident mitigating agency, said at a meeting in Jakarta.
"Tsunami didn't come alone, he was dragging cars, trees, houses with him, he knocked everything to the ground," Nugroho explained, adding that the tsunami traveled across the open sea at a speed of 800 km/h before hitting the coast.
He said that a few people climbed trees to escape the tsunami, and they survived.
Approximately 16,700 people were evacuated to 24 centers in Palu.
Aerial photographs published by the Accidents Agency show destroyed buildings and shops, twisted and demolished bridges, and a mosque surrounded by water.
Aftermath, aftershocks continued to shake the coastal city on Saturday.
In an area of 2.4 million people, a series of earthquakes were felt.
The Indonesian Agency for the Assessment and Use of Technology (BPPT) said in a statement that energy released during Friday's powerful earthquake is about 200 times higher than than that of an atomic bomb in Hiroshima in World War II.
The geography of the city, which lies at the end of a long and narrow bay, is expected to further increase the size of the tsunami.
Nugroho described the damage as “extensive” and said that thousands of houses, hospitals, shopping malls and hotels collapsed.
He also said that the bodies of some of the victims were found trapped under the ruins of the buildings, adding that 540 people had been injured and 29 were still missing.
Nugroho explained that the victims and the damage may be greater along the coast 300 km north of Palu, in the Donggal area, which is closer to the epicenter of the earthquake.
According to Nugroho, communications are completely interrupted and do not have any information from the Donggal area.
"More than 300,000 people live in the area," the Red Cross said in a statement.
“It’s a tragedy, but it can get worse,” they said.
On Saturday, the agency received a number of reviews as it did not inform the public that the tsunami had hit Palu, but officials say that the waves came just at the time of issuing a warning.
In an amateur video posted on social media, it is heard that a man on the top-floor is screaminging to warn people on the street on an approaching tsunami.
Within a few minutes, the water wall collided with the shore and took the buildings and cars with it.
At Reuters, they couldn’t immediately confirm the authenticity of the recording.
Earthquake and tsunami caused a power outage that disrupted communications around Palu, making it difficult for the authorities to coordinate rescue operations.
Authorities have reported that the military has started sending cargo planes with the help of Jakarta and other cities, but evacuated people still desperately need food and other basic necessities of life.
The city airport has only been opened to provide assistance and will remain closed until October.
Joko Widodo is expected to visit the Evacuation Centres in Palu on Sunday.
The death toll in the Indonesian tsunami has risen to more than 800 people.
- It's very bad.
