This hasn't happened yet.
“It’s still considered an accident at provincial level,” said Aulia Arriani, a spokeswoman of the Indonesian Red Cross.
“When the government says, ‘Okay, it’s a national disaster,’ we can accept international aid, but there’s no such status yet.”
On the second night after Friday's earthquake and tsunami in Palui, friends and relatives of the missing people clung to the hope that their close miracles lie in the sad stories of natural disasters.
On Saturday, a boy was pulled from the sewerage system.
On Sunday, the paramedics liberated a woman who had been trapped under the rubbles for two days, and her mother's corpse lay.
