U.S. Requires Doublish Artificial Intelligence Funding
As China becomes more active in the field of artificial intelligence, the U.S. should double the funds it spends on research in this area, says investor and artificial intelligence expert Kai-Fu Lee, who worked for Google, Microsoft and Apple.
Comments come after various parts of the U.S. government have announced the use of artificial intelligence, although the United States does not even have an official strategy for artificial intelligence.
In the meantime, China unveiled its plan last year: their goal is to become the best in artificial intelligence innovation by 2030.
“Doubling the AI research budget would be a good start, given that all other countries are pretty much for the U.S. and we’re looking for the next breakthrough in the field of artificial intelligence,” Lee said.
Doubling funding could double the chances that the next big achievement in artificial intelligence will take place in the U.S., Lee told CNBC in an interview this week.
Lee, whose book "AI Superpowers: China, Silicon Valley and the New World Order" was published this month by the publishing house Houghton Mifflin Harcourt, the president of Sinovation Ventures, which has invested in one of the most prominent artificial intelligence companies in China, Face++.
In the 1980’s Carnegie Mellon University developed an artificial intelligence system that defeated Othello’s highest-ranked U.S. player, and later served at Microsoft Research and chairman of Google’s Chinese subsidiary.
Lee said he’s paired out of previous U.S. government technology competitions, such as a robotics competition organized by DARPA (Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency), and asked when it would be next to make it easier to find the next visionaries.
U.S. researchers need to work hard if they want to receive state support, Lee said.
“China isn’t the one who takes over the best academics, it’s companies,” Lee said.
In recent years, Facebook, Google and other technology companies have recruited the best from universities to develop artificial intelligence.
Lee also said that changes to immigration policy could help the US to step up efforts in the field of artificial intelligence.
"I believe that people with a doctorate in artificial intelligence should automatically be offered a green card," he said.
In July 2017, the Chinese National Council issued a development plan for a new-generation artificial intelligence.
China’s National Science Foundation funds people in academia, much like the National Science Foundation and other government organizations distribute money to American explorers, but the quality of academic work in China is deteriorating, Lee explained.
Earlier this year, the U.S. Department of Defense established a Common Artificial Intelligence Center bringing together partners from the industrial and academia sectors, and the White House has announced the creation of a core committee on Artificial Intelligence.
This month, DARPA announced an investment in the $2 billion AI Next initiative.
National Science Foundation (NSF) currently invests more than $100 million per year in artificial intelligence research
Meanwhile, U.S. legislation, which wanted to create the National Security Commission on Artificial Intelligence, hasn’t done anything in months.
The Macedonians will decide in a referendum on changing the name of the state
Macedonian citizens voted in a referendum on Sunday to change their country's name to the "Republic of North Macedonia". This would dismiss a decades-long conflict with Greece, which opposed their application for membership of the European Union and NATO.
Greece, which has a province called Macedonia, insists that the name of its northern neighbour represents the right to their territory and has vetoed its accession to NATO and the EU.
In June, the governments reached an agreement based on the proposed new name, but nationalist opponents claim that the change would destroy the ethnic identity of the Slavic majority of the people of Macedonia.
President Gjorge Ivanov said he would not vote in the referendum, and the boycott campaign expressed doubts that the turnout would be at least 50 percent, as much as it was necessary for a referendum to be valid.
The question on the referendum was: “Are you in favour of membership of NATO and the EU with the adoption of an agreement with Greece?”
Supporters of the referendum, including Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, argue that this is a price that is worth paying for joining bodies such as the EU and NATO for Macedonia, one of the countries created after the disintegration of Yugoslavia.
“Today I have come to vote for the future of the country and for young people in Macedonia to live freely under the auspices of the European Union, because this means a safer life for all of us.”
Although it is not legally binding, enough members of the parliament have said that they will respect the outcome of the vote, which will be decisive.
Changing the name requires a two-thirds majority in parliament.
The National Electoral Commission announced that there were no irregularities reported by 13 o'clock.
However, the turnouncement was only 16% compared to 34 percent in the last parliamentary elections in 2016, when 66% of registered voters cast their vote.
“I’ve comede to vote because of my children, our city is in Europe,” said 62-year-old Gjose Tanevski, a voter from Skopja’s capital.
The Macedonian Prime Minister Zoran Zaev, his wife Zorica and his son Duško in Strumica in Macedonia on 30 September 2018 delivered their ballots for a referendum in Macedonia to change the name of the country, which would open the way to joining NATO and the European Union.
In front of the parliament in Skopje, 54-year-old Vladimir Kavardarkov prepared a small scaffolding and set up chairs in front of tents, which were set up by those who will boycott the referendum.
"We are for NATO and the EU, but we want to join with our heads raised, not through the back door," Said Kavadarkov.
“We are a poor country, but we have dignity.
If they do not want to accept us as Macedonia, we can turn to others, such as China and Russia, and become part of Eurasian integration.”
Prime Minister Zaev says NATO membership will bring a much-needed investment in Macedonia with an unemployment rate of more than 20 percent.
"I believe that there will be a large majority because more than 80 percent of our citizens are supported by the EU and NATO," Zaev said.
He added that a positive result would be a “confirmation of our future”.
A poll published last Monday by the Macedonian Politics Research Institute showed that between 30 and 43 percent of voters would take part in the referendum, which is less than the required voter turnout.
Another poll conducted by Telma television showed that 57% of respondents would vote on Sunday.
Of these, 70% would vote in favor.
In order for a referendum to succeed, voter turnout must be 50% plus one vote.
An unsuccessful referendum would represent the first more serious blow to the pro-Western government's policy, which took over last year's term of office in May.
