The Labour Party is no longer open to different views.
She is impatient with those who express her opinion.
When Momentum’s activist in my local party gave me a vote of no confidence, that wasn’t even a surprise.
After all, I am the last in a series of members of the parliament from the Labour Party, who are not desirable to express their opinions.
The same thing has happened to my fellow Member Joan Ryan because she resolutely resisted anti-Semitism.
In my case, it was a no-confidence motion to disagree with Jeremy Corbyn.
Regarding the importance of responsible economic policy, national security, Europe. Ironically similar issues on which Jeremy did not agree with the previous leaders.
According to Friday’s Nottingham East Laurist Party meeting, “we want meetings to be inclusive and productive.”
In the eight years that I am a member of the parliament of the local Labour Party, Friday's meetings were mostly exactly like that.
Today, unfortunately, such tons are not used at most meetings, and the promise of a “friendly and gentler” policy has long been forgotten if it has ever begun.
It is becoming increasingly obvious that different views are not admissible in the Labour Party and it is judged in every opinion whether this is acceptable for the leadership of the party.
This began shortly after Jeremy became a leader, and my colleagues, whom I previously thoughted to share similar political views, expected me to change my opinion and take positions with which I would otherwise never agree – be it in the area of national security or the EU single market.
When I speak publicly – and it really doesn’t matter what I’m saying – there are critiques on social networks calling for non-selection, judging middle-in-centre politics and telling me they shouldn’t be in the Labour Party.
And it's not just my experience.
In fact, I know that I am more fortunate than some of my colleagues, because the comments intended for me are just political.
I admire the professionalism and determination of those colleagues who daily face a flood of sexist or racist insults, but do not let themselves be frightened.
One of the most regrettable aspects of this era of politics is how rates of abuse have become quite normal.
Jeremy Corbyn explained last week that the Labour Party must promote a culture of tolerance.
But the reality is that we are no longer open to different opinions, and with each proposal of a censure or a change in the rules of choice, the client is becoming more narrow-minded.
In the last two years, I have received a lot of advice to bend my head and not to be so noisy, and “everything will be okay” with me.
But I didn't get into politics because of it.
Since 32 years ago, as a pupil, I joined the Labour Party – the reason for this was the negligence of Tatcher’s government, which literally made the classroom in my elementary school down – as a local councillor or government minister, I tried to promote better public services for those most in need.
I've never hid my political beliefs, including the last elections.
No one in the Nottingham East party could have been confused by my political views and areas on which I did not agree with the current leadership.
To those who supported the proposal on Friday, I just want to say that I do not understand their desire to waste time and energy to discuss my fidelity to the leader of the Labour Party at a time when the state is sailing against Brexit, which will affect households, businesses and our public services.
But my real message is not for Nottingham Momentum, but for my voters, regardless of whether they are members of the Labour Party or not: I am proud to have served you, and I promise that I will not be discouraged from acting in the best interest of all of you.
Chris Leslie is a Member of the Nottingham East Party
