“Sea is ours”: Bolivia, which does not have access to the sea, hopes that the court will reopen the way to the Pacific
Sailors patrol the Navy Headquarters in La Paz.
Navy blue flags are displayed on public buildings.
Maritime bases from Lake Titikaka to the Amazon are covered with the slogan: “We have the right to the sea.
It’s our duty to repatriate it.”
In mainland Bolivia, the memory of the loss of the coastal in a bloody conflict due to natural resources with Chile in the 19 th century is still alive – just like the craving for recurrence sailing the Pacific Ocean.
Bolivia is awaiting the decision of the International Court of Justice, which will be received after five years of consultations on 1 October.
“Bolivia has an impetus, a spirit of unity and impartiality and, of course, in a positive spirit expects a result,” said Roberto Calzadilla, Bolivian diplomat.
Many Bolivians will look at an international court’s decision on big screens across the country and hope that a court in The Hague will rule in favour of Bolivia, claiming that – after decades of volatile talks – Chile must negotiate Bolivia’s access to sovereign access to the sea.
Evo Morales, Bolivian charismatic indigenous president who faces a controversial battle for re-election next year, also has high expectations for Monday's verdict.
“We’re very close to returning to the Pacific Ocean,” he promised at the end of August.
But some analysts believe the court won’t decide in favour of Bolivia — and even if it did, it wouldnn’t change much.
A UN body based in the Netherlands does not have the power to allocate Chilean territory to Bolivia and will not decide on the outcome of possible talks.
The fact that the decision of an international court comes only six months after hearing the final arguments means that the case was “not complicated,” said Paz Zárate, a Chilean expert on international law.
And in the last four years they haven't spoken in favor of Bolivia, they'll maybe cost her a lot.
“The Current Bolivian government has taken advantage of the issue of access to the sea,” Zarate said.
Morales' aggressive rhetoric sucked from the Chileans all the good will that may have remained, she explained.
Conversations between Bolivia and Chile will continue at some point, but after that it will be extremely difficult to participate in discussions.
They haven't exchanged ambassadors since 1962.
Former President Eduardo Rodriguez Veltzé, Bolivian spokesmaker in The Hague, rejected the idea that the court had made an unusual decision quickly.
Monday will bring Bolivia an "extraordinary opportunity for a new era of relations with Chile" and an opportunity to "complete 139-year disagreements with mutual benefits," he said.
Calzadilla also denied Morales – still one of Latin America’s most popular presidents – used the naval issue as a political support.
“Bolivia will never renounce its right of access to the Pacific Ocean,” he said.
“The decision is an opportunity to see that we must overcome the past.”
North Korea says nuclear disarmament won't happen if it can't trust the U.S.
North Korean Foreign Minister Ri Yong Ho said his nation would never be the first to destroy his nuclear weapons if they couldn’t trust Washington.
Ri gave a speech at the United Nations General Assembly on Saturday.
He urged the United States to deliver on their promises made during a meeting between the leaders of the rival countries in Singapore.
His statement comes at a time when U.S. Secretary Mike Pompeo is apparently more than three months ahead of relaunching nuclear diplomacy, which was at a standstill, more than three months after meeting North Korean leader Kim Jong Un in Singapore.
Ri says the U.S. is mistaken if they think that the continuation of sanctions and an objection will bring North Korea to its knees.
Washington is cautious in accompanying the announcement, without Pyongyang first takining resolute measures for disarmament.
Kim and U.S. President Donald Trump want a second meeting.
But there is general doubt that Pyongyang is serious about renouncing weapons that the state sees as the only way to ensure its safety.
Pompeo plans to visit Pyongyang next month to prepare everything for Kim and Trump’s second meeting.
