John Edward: Knowledge of Languages is Crucial to Global Citizens
The Scottish Solo Schools enjoy the reputation of academic excellence, and this continued in 2018 with another set of outstanding results in the exams, which are further strengthened by individual and collective successes in sports, art, music and other efforts of the community.
With more than 30,000 students throughout Scotland, these schools, represented by the Scottish Independent School Council (SCIS), strive to provide the highest level of service for their students and their parents.
Independent schools want to prepare their students for further and higher education, a chosen career path and a place they have as global citizens.
As an education sector that can design and implement an adapted school program, we see that modern languages are still a popular and desired elective subject in schools.
Nelson Mandela said, “If you talk to a man in a language he understands, the conversation is imprinted in his memory.
However, if you talk to a man in his mother tongue, the words are imprinted on his heart.”
It’s a good reminder that we shouldn’t rely solely on English when we want to build relationships and trust with people from other countries.
This year’s recent exam results show that languages are at the top of the ranking with the highest success rates in independent schools.
68% of students who learned a foreign language achieved an excellent grade.
Data collected in 74 schools of the Scottish Independence Council (SCIS), showed that 72 percent of students achieved an excellent grade in Mandarin, while 72 percent of German students, 69% and 63% of Spanish students also received an excellent grade.
This proves that independent schools in Scotland support foreign languages as a key knowledge that children and young people will undoubtedly need in the future.
Languages are treated as an elective course in the curricula of independent schools and elsewhere in the same way as subjects in the fields of natural sciences, technology, engineering and mathematics.
A survey carried out by the United Kingdom Employment and Training Commission in 2014 showed that the reasons why employers struggle to fill vacancies can attribute 17% to the lack of language proficiency.
As a result, language skills are becoming increasingly necessary in preparing young people for their future careers.
For future job opportunities that require language skills, these skills are essential in the global world.
If one learns another language, regardless of the career path he chooses, with such lifelong knowledge, he has a great advantage in the future.
A multilingual person who can directly communicate with people from foreign countries has an automatic advantage over the competition.
A YouGov poll of 2013, involving more than 4000 adults in the UK, showed that 75 percent of people didn’t speak a foreign language well enough to be able to talk, and French was the only language spoken by a two-digit percentage of respondents, that’s 15%.
Therefore, investing in language learning is important for today's children.
With the knowledge of several languages, especially those from countries with a developing economy, children have a better chance of finding a suitable job.
In Scotland, schools differ in the languages they teach.
Numerous schools focus on more classical modern languages, while others teach languages that are expected to be most important for the United Kingdom in 2020, such as Mandarin or Japanese.
Regardless of the interests of your child, it will always be possible to choose a number of languages taught by specialists in this field.
Scottish independent schools are dedicated to providing a learning environment that will prepare and armament children with the knowledge they need to succeed, no matter what the future holds.
In the global business environment, it cannot be denied that languages remain extremely important for the future of the country, so this must also be expressed in education.
In fact, modern languages should be regarded as "international communication skills".
Independent schools will continue to offer this choice, diversity and excellence for young people in Scotland.
Il faut bien only faire.
John Edward is Director of the Scottish Board of Independent Schools
