But after two days in a different place, Sunday's counter-attack may seem too big for her.
North Korea: “Will not be unilaterally disarmed without trust”
North Korea’s foreign secretariat said on Saturday that the continued imposition of sanctions leads to increasing distrust in the United States of America and that North Korea will certainly not unilaterally renounce its nuclear weapons under such conditions.
Ri Jong Ho said at an annual meeting of the General Assembly of this global body that in the past year North Korea has taken a number of “important measures as a sign of goodwill”, such as halting the implementation of nuclear and missile tests, demolishing the city for nuclear tests and promising not to spread nuclear weapons and technology.
“Nevertheless, we don’t see an adequate response from the United States,” he said.
“Without trust in the United States, we will not be sure of national security, and under such conditions we will by no means unilaterally disarm ourselves first.”
Whilst Ri reiterated North Korea’s well-knowneded complaints about Washington’s reluctance to a step-by-step approach to nuclear disarmament, under which North Korea would be rewarded for taking step-by-step measures, his statement was important because he did not immediately dismiss a unilateral nuclear disarmament, as Pyongyang did in the past in the past.
Ri referred to Kim Jong Un and Donald Trump's joint statement from the first summit between the current U.S. president and North Korean leader, which took place in Singapore on June 12. At that time, Kim undertook to strive for a "nuclear disarmament of the Korean Peninsula", while Trump pledged security assurances to North Korea.
North Korea wants an official closure of the Korean War, which took place between 1950 and 1953, but the United States said Pyongyang must renounce nuclear weapons first.
Washington also did not indulge calls to unleash strict international sanctions imposed on North Korea.
“The United States insists that we first have to disarm it nuclear, and put pressure on sanctions in order to compulsion to achieve our goals, as well as pronouncing an end to the war,” he said.
“In the thought that sanctions could put us on our knees, it’s an unrealistical hope for people who don’t even know us.
However, the problem is that the continued imposition of sanctions exacerbates our mistrust."
Ri did not mention plans for the second summit between Kimo and Trump, which the U.S. president pointed out at the United Nations Assembly earlier this week.
The Minister instead emphasised three meetings between Kim and South Korean President Mun Dže In, which took place over the past five months, adding: “If we were to talk with South Korea and not the United States about the problem of nuclear disarmament, nuclear disarmament of the Korean Peninsula would not have landed at such a deadlock.”
Nevertheless, the tone of Rie’s speech was completely different from last year, when he told the United Nations General Assembly that an attack on the continental part of the U.S. with North Korean missiles was unavoidable after “Mistress Evil President” Trump called Kim a “missible man” on a suicidal mission.
This year, Trump, threatening to “completely destroy” North Korea last year, praised Kim and his courage in taking measures to disarmament, but said he still had a lot of work to do and that sanctions should remain valid until North Korea disarmamed.
On Wednesday, Trump said there was no timeframe for this, saying: “It can last two years, three years or five months – that doesn’t matter.”
China and Russia believe the UN Security Council should reward Pyongyang for the measures taken.
On Thursday, U.S. Secretary of State Mike Pompeo said to the United Nations Security Council: “Enforcementation of sanctions by the Security Council must resolutely continue without exception until we recognize the complete, definitive and proven state of nuclear disarmament.”
Since 2006, the Security Council has unanimously imposed additional sanctions on North Korea in order to block the financing of North Korean programs for nuclear weapons and ballistic missiles.
Pompeo and Ri met on the sidelines of the United Nations General Assembly and later said Pyongyang would visit again next month to prepare for the second summit.
Pompeo has visited North Korea three times this year, but his last visit didn’t end well.
Leaving Pyongyang in July, he stated that there had been progress, and North Korea criticized him a few hours later that his demands were “gangster”.
At a meeting with Mun this month, North Korea pledged to tear down missile testing stations and infrastructure for nuclear tests if the United States takes “adequate measures”.
Munu is said to have said that “adequate measures” mean security assurances promised by Trump in Singapore, as well as measures to normalize relations with Washington.
Harvard University Students Attend a Course to Teach How to Take Plenty of Time to Rest
The goal of this year's Harvard University course is to teach all undergraduate students how to indulge in more sleep. With such a course, they want to combat the growing macho culture of "comprehensive" learning, in which power is accumulated with caffeine.
The university professor found that students of the world's best university often do not know the basics of taking care of themselves.
Charles Czeisler, a professor of sleep medicine at the Harvard Medical School and a specialist at the Brigham Women's Hospital, designed the course, which, according to him, is the first of its kind in the United States.
For the implementation of such a course, he decided after his lecture on the impact of a lack of sleep on learning.
At the end of the lecture, the girl approached him and said: “Why do I only find out now that I am in my senior year?”
She said no one had ever told her how important it was to have a night's sleep, and the professor told The Daily Telegraph that surprised him.
The course, carried out for the first time this year, speaks about the crucial importance of good sleeping habits for study and sports success, as well as for improving overall well-being.
Paul Barreira, a professor of psychiatry at the Harvard Medical School and the university’s executive director of medical services, says they decided to take such a course when they found out that students lack sleep significantly during the week.
The one-hour course includes a number of interactive tasks.
In one section there is a picture of the dormitory doors, where students can click coffee cups, curtains, sneakers and books to obtain information about the influence of caffeine and light, the impact of sleep deprivation on sports success and the importance of sleeping routine.
In the second section, however, participants learn that long-term sleep deprivation can increase the risk of cardiac arrest, stroke, depression, and cancer.
With interactive icons, a map of the student settlement encourages participants to think about their daily routine.
"We are aware that students' behavior will not change immediately.
However, we believe that they have the right to familiarize themselves with these facts, just as they have the right to know how cigarette smoking affects their health," Czeisler adds.
The professor says that the culture of pride is still present with “surviving all-night learning”. He also adds that modern technology and the growing pressure on students, resulting in a lack of sleep, represent a growing problem.
Professor Czeisler says that a sufficient amount of high-quality sleep should be a “secret weapon” of students to combat stress, exhaustion and anxiety. Thus, they can also avoid gaining weight – the brain, due to lack of sleep, passes into a way of hunger, making students constantly hungry.
A 19-year-old Calyfornian chemistry and physical biology student Raymond So helped Professor Czeisler to design a course. Last year, during his first year at Harvard, he attended one of the professor's lectures.
He says that the lecture opened his eyes and encouraged him to carry out the course throughout the student settlement.
He hopes that in the next stage he will be able to ask postgraduate students to complete a similar study program before enrolling in a competitive institution.
Professor Czeisler recommends students to set up an alarm clock for sleeping as well as waking up. He also wants to warn them about the harmful effects of “blue light” emitted by electronic screens and LED lighting, as they can cause an imbalance of the circadian rhythm, which makes it difficult to fall asleep.
