Almanah: The Inventor of the Geiger Counter
Introducing a page from the almanac of our Sunday editions: September 30, 1882, that i.e. more than 136 years ago, Johannes Wilhelm "Hans" Geiger was born in Germany, which later became a world-famous physicist.
Geiger developed the method of detecting and measuring radioactivity, this invention was the basis for the development of a device called the Geiger counter.
It became a constant in the scientific world and also gained its place in pop culture, such as the 1950 film "Bells of Coronado", in which Roy Rogers and Dale Evans play cowboy scientists:
Man: "What's the hell?"
Rogers: "It's Geiger's counter. It's used to find radioactive minerals such as uranium.
When you put on your headphones, you can hear the effect it has on atoms radioactivity in materials.”
Evans: "Madonna, they're really answering now!"
"Hans" Geiger died in 1945, just a few days before his 63rd birthday.
Invention with his name lives forward.
New Cancer Vaccine Can Teach the Immune System Recognize Attacking Cells
New Cancer Vaccine Can Teach the Immune System to Recognize Attacking Cells and Destroy Them
Vaccine in therapy teaches the immune system to recognize attack cells
This method involves the acquisition of the patient's immune cells, which are then changed in the laboratory
After that, they can “see” the protein that is characteristic of many forms of cancer, then they are returned to the patient
The results of the test vaccine are very encouraging in patients with a wide variety of cancers.
In one of the women who received the vaccine, which teaches the immune system to recognize the attacking cells, ovarian cancer disappeared for more than 18 months.
This method involves obtaining the patient's immune cells, changing them in the laboratory so that they can “see” the HER2 protein, which is characteristic of many forms of cancer, and then re-injected.
Professor Jay Berzofsky of the American National Institute for Cancer Research in Bethesda, Maryland, said: “Our results show that we have a very promising vaccine.”
HER2 “promotes the growth of several different types of cancer,” including breast, ovarian, lung and colon cancer.
A similar approach of obtaining immune cells of patients “teached” to target cancer cells also worked in the treatment of the type of leukemia.
