HIV/aids: 14% increase in new cases reported from China
China has announced a 14% increase in the number of people living with HIV and AIDS.
Health officials say more than 820,000 people in the country are affected.
Approximately 40,000 new cases were reported in the second quarter of 2018 alone.
In the vast majority of new cases, the disease was transferred through sexual intercourse, which represents a change compared to the past.
Usually, the HIV virus spread rapidly in some parts of China due to transfusion of infected blood.
However, according to Chinese health officials from a conference in Junan province, the number of people infected with HIV in this way has decreased to almost zero.
However, the number of people living with HIV and AIDS is still increasing by 100,000 people per year in China.
Carriage of HIV through sexual intercourse is a very pressing problem in the Chinese LGBT community.
Homosexuality was decriminalized in China in 1997, but discrimination against people from the LGBT community is still very present.
Since there are still conservative values in the country, according to the results of research, 70–90% of men who have sex with men eventually marry a woman.
The disease is often transmitted due to inadequate protection in sexual intercourse in such proportions.
Since 2003, as part of an effort to solve the problem, the Chinese government has pledged universal access to HIV medicines.
Maxine Waters denies that staff has released information about Republican senators, condemns ‘dangerous lies’ and ‘conspiracy theories’
U.S. Congresswoman Maxine Waters on Saturday declined allegations that a member of her staff had published information about three U.S. Republican senators on their websites in Wikipedia.
A Democrat in Los Angeles said these accusations come from individuals and far-right websites.
"Lies, lies and more inadmissible lies," Waters said in a statement on Twitter.
Published information, according to statements, includes the domestic addresses and phone numbers of U.S. Senators Lindsey Graham of South Carolina and Mike Lee and Orrin Hatcha of Utah.
