Information leaks occurred after the three senators had finished interrogating Kavanaugh.
Conservative sites such as Gateway Pundit and RedState state that a source of publication associated with Senator Waters' office can be identified via an IP address and conclude that the information was published by one of the staff.
"These unfounded accusations are a completely false and sheer lie" continues Waters.
“My employees, whose identity, personal data and security are compromised by these false allegations, are in no way responsible for the leakage of this data.
These unjustified accusations are completely untrue and pure lies.”
Senator Waters’ statement quickly found criticism on the internet, including former White House spokeswoman Ari Fleischer.
“From this denial of the branch of anger,” Fleischer wrote.
“It’s not from a real congressional test.
When you accuse someone of something he didn't do, he shouldn't show anger.
She can't resist.
And he can't question the motive of the one he's accused.
He has to stay calm.”
Fleischer compared Waters' response to Democratic criticism of Judge Kavanaugh, who was accused of being too clearly angry during Thursday's interrogation.
Omar Navarro, a Republican candidate who is competing for the position of Waters in the mid-term elections, tweeted.
“If that’s true, it’s serious,” he tweeted.
Waters stated in a statement that her cabinet had informed “the relevant institutions and law enforcement authorities in connection with these false accusations.
“We’re going to make sure the perpetrators are exposed,” she continued, “and will be held accountable for all the acts that are harmful and dangerous to every member of my staff and all of them.”
Johnny English in action - Rowan Atkinson as a covert agent
Finding tips on Brexit in any of Britain’s new films has become a tradition, and it’s also considered the revival of the action comedy Johnny English, which first came to the screen in 2003 with the title of Johnny English and the sequel in 2011 titled Johnny English 2.
Will it be a funerous satire about how bad we are, a new export opportunity for us?
Uncompetent Johnny English with bulging eyes and unusual grimasses obtains permission to shuffles again; already his name suggests that it is a comedy character created for a cinematic audience in non-English-speaking areas.
It is an awkward secret agent who, despite his bizarre gestures, contains part of Clouseau, a hint of Mr. Bean and a trifle that contributed the last note to Chariots of Fire at the ceremony at the opening of the 2012 Olympic Games in London.
It’s also based on a traveler and an international mystery sanger that Atkinson once played in Barclaycard’s now-forgotten TV ad, leaving behind a real chaos.
In the latest movie, one or two pleasant moments are presented.
I liked it when Johnny English approached the helicopter, he wore medieval armor, and the leaves of the rotor briefly hit his helmet.
Atkinson's gift for comedy is clearly portrayed, but humor seems somewhat mild and excessively unnecessary, especially since “serious” films such as 007 or Mission Impossible now offer comedy elements without a guilty conscience.
Humour seems to be aimed at children and not so many adult spectators, which is precisely why Johnny English's repulsive adventures are not as innovative and oriented as Atkinson's mute sketches in the role of Mr. Bean.
It is now obvious that Britain is in serious trouble.
An online hacker infiltrated an online network of Britain's most secret agents and revealed the identities of all British agents, causing great trouble to a competent agent represented by Kevin Eldon in a surprisingly small role.
