The intelligence service informs her that all active spies have been exposed, so she must opt for one of the retired spies.
And this means Johnny English, now a teacher at a prestigious school who unofficially teaches how to act undercover: this is where the screenwriters scored, as English performs a kind of spy “academy of rock”.
They're taken to Whitehall for an emergency meeting, where she meet his longtiming co-worker Bough, played by Ben Miller.
Bough is now married to submarine commander, stereotypical sports enthusiast, a role in which Vicki Pepperdine gets lost quite a bit.
Batman and Robin are ready to create even more problems in the name of her Majesty's secret service, meeting the fatale Ophelio Bullet, played by Olga Kurylenko.
In the meantime, the prime minister dangerously falls under the influence of a charismatic tech billionaire who claims she can solve Britain’s computer problems: Jason Volta, an evil, played by Jake Lacy.
English and Bough begin their odyssey: dressed as a waiter, they frame a fire at a French restaurant, creating chaos as they smuggle on board the Volt yacht, and when English tries to use virtual reality glasses to get acquainted with the interior of Volt's house, he manages to create general chaos.
Although the last part contains all the necessary ingredients, despite the sympathy and energy it resembles a children's film.
Very mediocre.
As with other Johnny English movies, I can’t help it, and I don’t think, ‘Is the British film capable of giving Atkinson a role that actually justifies his talent?
Labourists denie that a four-day working week is planned for the British, receiving payment for a five-day working week.
