What is Global Citizen Festival and has it contributed in any way to reducing poverty?
This Saturday, New York will host the Global Citizens Festival, an annual music event attended by a truly extraordinary series of stars and has the same extraordinary mission: to eradicate poverty in the world.
The festival, which will take place for the seventh time, will be attended by tens of thousands of people. They will occupy the Great Lawn of the Central Park, not only to enjoy the performances of musicians such as Janet Jackson, Cardi B and Shawn Mendes, but also to arouse interest in the true purpose of the event, i.e. the eradication of extreme poverty by 2030.
The Festival of Global Citizens, launched in 2012, is an extension of the Global Citizens Project, an international advocacy group that hopes to eliminate poverty by increasing the number of people actively fighting against it.
In order to receive a vacant ticket for the event (unless you were willing to pay for a VIP-ticket), the attendees of the concert had to complete a series of tasks or “actions”, such as volunteering, a letter to a world leader, a phone call or any other sensible way to raise awareness of their goal of eliminating poverty.
How successful is the Global Citizen movement, which has remained for 12 years to achieve its goal?
Is the idea of rewarding people with a free concert the right way to persuade them to require action, or is this just another example of so-called “clictivism”—creating the belief that people actually have a significant impact on the situation by signing an online petition or passing a tweet?
The Global Citizen movement states that more than 19 million “actions” of its supporters have been recorded since 2011 in pursuit of a number of different goals.
“These actions have helped world leaders make commitments and policies worth more than $37 billion, which is expected to affect the lives of more than 2.25 billion people by 2030.
Early in 2018, the group stated that it was the result of 390 commitments and messages, on the basis of which at least $10 billion had already been disbursed or collected.
The group estimates that the funds raised so far have had a direct impact on almost 649 million people around the world.
Some of the key commitments are The Power of Nutrition, a partnership between investors and decision-makers committed to “helping children realize their full potential,” who pledged $35 million in aid to Rwanda to help eliminate malnutrition after receiving more than 4700 tweets of the Global Citizens movement.
“Through the support of the United Kingdom government, donors, national governments and global citizens like you, we can put the social injustice of malnutrition into historical archives,” Tracey Ullman, Ambassador for the Power of Nutrition Partnership, told the crowd at a concert in London in April 2018.
The group also stated that, after more than 5000 actions have been carried out calling on the United Kingdom to improve the nutrition of mothers and children, the government has announced that it will co-finance the Power of Nutrition project, which will reach 5 million women and children through nutritional measures.
In response to one of the frequently asked questions on the site, “Why do you think we can eliminate extreme poverty?”,
the Global Citizen movement replied: “It will be a long and hard road – sometimes we will fall and we will fail.
However, like the great civil rights movement and the fight against apartheid before us, we will succeed, because together we are stronger.
Some of the performers who will perform at this year's event in New York are Janet Jackson, the Weeknd, Shawn Mendes, Cardi B and Janelle Monáe. The event will be hosted by Deborra-Lee Furness and Hugh Jackman.
The United States Could Use the Navy to “Block” Russian Energy Exports – U.S. Interior Ministry
U.S. Interior Minister Ryan Zinke said Washington Examiner said Washington could use the navy to prevent Russia’s energy marketing, including in the Middle East.
Zinke said that Russian intervention in Syria (it should be noted that at the invitation of a legitimate government) is an excuse to explore the new energy markets.
"I believe that the reason for their presence in the Middle East is the desire to market energy, as it is marketed in Eastern Europe, in the southern part of Europe," he said.
According to the official, however, there are ways and means to cope with this.
“United States can do this. The Navy makes sure that our maritime routes are open and, if necessary, block them, thus preventing Russian energy products from flowing into the market,” he said.
Zinke turned to participants in an event hosted by the Energy Consumers' Association, a non-profit group that is referred to as "the voice of energy consumers" in the United States.
He compared Washington's approach to Russia and Iran, pointinging that it was actually the same.
“As far as Iran and Russia are concerned, the economic possibility is more or less influencing and replacing fuels,” he said, calling Russia a “pony that knows one trick” with a fossil fuel-dependent economy.
Statements have been made at a time when the Trump administration is trying to increase exports of its liquefied natural gas to Europe, where it is supposed to supplant Russia, which is a significantly cheaper choice for European consumers.
To this end, officials of Trump’s government, including the U.S. President themselves, Donald Trump, are trying to persuade Germany to renounce the “inappropriate” Nord Stream 2 gas pipeline project, which, according to Trump, Berlin is believed to be the “halt” of Moscow.
Moscow has repeatedly pointed out that the Nord Stream 2 gas pipeline is worth $11 billion, which is expected to double the existing gas pipeline capacity to 110 billion cubic meters, an exclusively economic project.
Kremlin argues that Washington strongly opposes the project only for economic reasons and that this is unfair competition.
"In my opinion, we have the same view that energy should not be a tool to exert pressure and that consumers must have the right to choose suppliers," Russian Energy Minister Aleksander Novak said.
Germany dismissed the U.S. position and confirmed its commitment to the project.
Germany’s leading industrial organisation, the Union of German Industries (BDIs), has urged the United States not to interfere in the EU’s energy policy and bilateral agreements between Berlin and Moscow.
“Integrating a third country into our energy supply is a big problem for me,” said Dieter Kempf, Head of the German Industry (BDI).
