U.S. Ambassador Accuses China's 'Harassing' With 'Propaganda Ads'
One week after the publication of a four-sided advertisement by an official Chinese newspaper in the U.S. diary praising the reciprocal benefits of trade between the United States and China, the U.S. ambassador to China accused Beijing of abusing the U.S. press to spread propaganda.
U.S. President Donald Trump last Sunday referred to a paid addition to the China Daily newspaper in the Des Moines Registry – the best-selling newspaper in Iowa after accusing China of interfering in the U.S. congressional election on November 6, which China denies.
Reuters reported that U.S. federal officials described Trump’s accusation that Beijing had attempted to interfere in the U.S. federal elections as a new phase in Washington’s intense efforts to put pressure on China.
Although it’s common for foreign governments to publish trade-promoting ads, Beijing and Washington are currently complicated into an increasingly intense trade war in which they exchange blows by imposing tariffs on import products.
China’s retaliatory tariffs at the beginning of a trade war allegedly hit exporters in states such as Iowa supporting the Trump Republican Party, Chinese and U.S. experts said.
Terry Branstad, U.S. ambassador to China and former long-time governor Iowa, the main exporter of agricultural goods to China, said Beijing had hit U.S. workers, farmers and businesses.
China, Branstad wrote in a column in a Sunday edition of Des Moines Register, “now this harassment is duplicated by publishing propaganda advertisements in our own free press.”
“In disseminating propaganda, the Chinese government took advantage of the cherished American tradition of freedom of speech and the free press to publish a paid advertisement in the Des Moines Registry,” Branstad wrote.
“On the other hand, you’ll see only a few other opinions on the newspaper stands in Beijing, and no real reflections of the diversity of opinions that Chinese citizens have about China’s alarming economic path, as the media is strictly controlled by the Chinese Communist Party,” he wrote.
He added that "one of the most important Chinese newspapers rejected the bid for publication" of his article, although he did not specify which one.
