The inventor of the World Wide Web plans to start a new Internet in order to cope with Google and Facebook
Tim Berners-Lee, the inventor of the World Wide Web, opens a start-up that will compete with Facebook, Amazon and Google.
The last project of this technological legend, Inrupt, is a company that builds on Berners' open-source Solid platform.
Solid allows users to choose where their data is stored and who has access to any information.
In an exclusive interview with the Fast Company, Berners-Lee joked that Inrupt’s purpose was “to rule the world”.
“We’ve decided to do it now,” he said.
“It’s a historic moment.”
The application uses Solid platform technology to allow users to create a "personal repository for data storage" or POD
This can contain contact information, task lists, calendar, music library, and other personal and professional tools.
It’s like Google Drive, Microsoft Outlook, Slack and Spotify all available in one browser and at the same time.
The peculiarity of the personal data storage repository is that the user can fully determine who can access any information.
The company calls this “Personal Empowerment with Data”.
CEO John Bruce says the company’s idea is to combine resources, processes and relevant skills that will give everyone access to the Solid platform.
The company currently consists of Berners-Lee, Bruce, a security platform purchased by IBM, a few hired developers to work on the project and a community of volunteer programmers.
Already this week, technology developers around the world, using the tools available on Inrupt's website, could create their own decentralized applications.
Berners-Lee said he and the group “don’t talk to Facebook and Google about whether they could make a complete change that could shut down all their business models overnight.”
“We don’t ask permission.”
Berners-Lee wrote in a Saturday's announcement on Medium that Inrupt's intention was to "ensure commercial energy and an ecosystem to protect the integrity and quality of the new web built on the Solid platform."
In 1994, Berners-Lee transformed the Internet with the establishment of the World Wide Web Consortium at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology (MIT).
In recent months, Berners-Lee has been an influential voice in the debate on net neutrality.
Berners-Lee will remain the founder and director of the World Wide Web Consortium consortium, the World Wide Web Foundation and the Open Data Institute.
“I’m extremely optimistic about the next era of the web,” Berners-Lee added.
Bernard Vann: Celebrator of the Victoria Cross from World War 1
For 100 years, their home town has celebrated the awarding of the Victorian Cross to the only priest of the English Church, which he acquired as a fighter in the First World War.
Venerable lieutenant Colonel Bernard Vann deserved recognition on September 29, 1918 in an attack on Bellenglise and Lehaucourt.
But four days later, a sniper shot him, so he never found out that he had been granted this highest British military honor.
On Saturday, at a parade in Rushden, Northamptonshire, two colonel's grandchildren unveiled a commemorative stone.
One of the great-grandchildren, Michael Vann, said it was “extremely symbolic” that the stone was discovered exactly 100 years after his grandfather’s action earned a medalulation.
According to the London Gazette newspaper, Lieutenant Colonel Vann led his battalion on September 29, 1918, through the Canal of Saint Quintin "through a very dense fog and under the strong fire of the battlefield and machine guides."
Subsequently, he rushed towards the firing line and with the "greatest heroism" led the line forward, and then attacked the engine room himself and scored three detachment members.
On October 4, 20101 – just a month before the end of the war – Lieutenant Colonel Vann was shot by a German sniper.
Michael Vann, 72, said the actions of my grandfather were “something I know and could never achieve, so he teaches me humility.”
He and his brother, Dr. James Vann, laid a wreath after a parade led by Brentwood Imperial Youth Band.
Michael Vann said that he felt “very honoured to be able to participate in this parade,” and “that the bravity of the real hero is reflected in the support that many people will say.”
Enthusiasts of mixed martial arts watched all night to watch Bellator 206. Instead, they received the Pepe Pigs.
Imagine: You've been watching all night all night to watch a completely sold-out Bellator 206, and then you can't see the main event.
The San Jose event consisted of 13 matches, including six on the main parquet, and was broadcast live in the United Kingdom on Channel 5.
At 6 a.m., when Gegard Mousasi and Rory MacDonald were preparing to cope, viewers in the United Kingdom were fascinated to find that the transmission had been replaced by the playback of Pepa's cartoon.
A lot of people weren’t thrilled because he stayed awake until the morning because of the fight.
One of the enthusiasts on Twitter described switching to a children's cartoon as "a kind of sick joke".
“According to the government regulation, this content is unsuitable at 6 a.m., so they had to switch to a children’s program,” said Dave Schwartz, vice-president of Bellator’s marketing and communication organization, when asked about the transfer.
“Peppa’s Pig. Yes.”
Bellator president Scott Coker said he was trying to adjust the schedule in such a way that viewers in the United Kingdom would also be involved in the future.
“When I’m thinking about replaying, I think we could take care of it,” Coker said.
“But it’s six o'clock in the morning on Sunday, and we won’t be able to fix it until Sunday after our time or Monday.
But we're working on it.
Believe me, there were many messages when switching and that not everyone was nice.
We tried to fix it; we assumed it was just a technical error.
However, the actual reason was a government regulation.
I can promise you this won't happen again next time.
Instead of six, we will endure five duels – as usual. We tried to offer our fans more than expectations and we counted.
It’s a very ungrateful situation.”
Dessert Island Discs: Tom Daley felt “inferior” about sex.
Olympic diver Tom Daley said he felt inferior to anyone because of his sexual orientation—but it motivated him to succeed.
The 24-year-old said that “not everyone is like himself” was deceived when entering high school.
During an interview at the first Desert Island Discs hosted by Lauren Laverne for Radio 4, he said he had spoken about gay rights to give others “hope.”
He also said that when he became a parent, his desire to win the Olympics was diminished.
A full-time presenter, Kirsty Young, has been absented due to several months of illness.
Daley, who appeared as an outlaw on Laverneo’s first show, said he felt “inferior” than everyone else, because “it wasn’t socially acceptable to love boys and girlfriends.”
“To this day, these feelings of inferiority and difference are an actual thing that gives me the strength and perseverance to achieve success.”
He wanted to prove that he was “something,” he said he wouldn’t disappoint anyone who would finally learn about his orientation.
Twice-bronze Olympic contestant became an influential supporter of LGBT rights and this time appeared twice at the Nations Community Games in Australia to urge countries to decriminalize homosexuality.
He said he spoke because he felt happy that he could speak openly without consequences, and that he wanted to give “hope” to others.
A three-time world champion said he was “surprised by the romance with the man – American filmmaker Dustin Lance Black, whom he met in 2013.”
Daley married an Oscar winner who was 20 years older than him last year, but he says the age difference never meant a problem.
“When young people experience so much” – at the age of 14 he attended the First Olympic Games, and three years later his father died – he said it’s hard to find someone of the same age who would experience similar ups and downs.
The couple adopted a child in June, Robert Ray Black-Daley’s son, and Daley says it’s changed his “full view.”
“If I had been asked last year, it would have been all I had to say, ‘I had to achieve a gold medal,’” he said.
“But, you know, there’s bigger things in the world than an Olympic gold medal.
"My Olympian gold medal is Robbie."
Daley's father Robert, who died in 2011 at the age of 40 because of brain cancer.
Daley said his father didn’t accept that he was going to have to die, and one of his last things he asked about was if he already had tickets to London 2012, because he wanted to sit in the front row.
I couldn’t tell him, “You won’t be in the first place this time, Daddy,” he said.
“I held his hand when he stopped breathing, and it wasn’t only when he actually stopped breathing and he was dead that I finally had to admit that he wasn’t invulnerable,” he said.
The following year, Daley competed in the 2012 Olympics and achieved a bronze medal.
“I knew it was what I’ve been dreaming about all my life—diving at the Olympics in front of a domestic audience. There wasn’t a better feeling,” he said.
This was also inspired by his first selection of the song – Proud musician Heather Smalt, who accompanied him in preparation for the Olympics and still makes his skin creep.
Desert Island Discs broadcast on BBC Radio 4 every Sunday at 11:15 (BST).
Unprepared Mickelson Sifted the Ryder Cup on Saturday
American Phil Mickelson will set a record on Sunday, when he plays his 47th game in the Ryder Cup, but he will have to get back in shape again so that it doesn’t become an unfortunate milestone.
Mickelson, who will participate in the biennal event for a record 12th time, let Captain Furyk sit on Saturday's quadruples.
Instead of being at the heart of what was so often for the United States, the five-timer winner spent the day partly as a fan and partly in improving his game in order to fix what weighs on him.
Considering that even at the height of his career he did not measure the most straight, the 48-year-old does not fit the very narrow Le Golf National, where a long ice inevitably penalises misguided kicks.
And if only the field wasn’t intimidating enough, Mickelson will be dealing with the exact winner of the British Open Francesco Molinari Cup in the ninth game this weed to team up with Tommy Fleetwood.
If the Americans, who have four games less at the beginning of 12 games of individuals, got off to a good start, Mickelson's match could turn out to be decisive.
Furyk expressed confidence in his man, but he didn't say much.
“He completely understands the role he’d played today, he pats me on my back and hugged me with his hand and said he’d be ready tomorrow,” Furyk said.
“He has a lot of confidence in himself.
He is one of the celebrities and in the past and this week greatly contributed to the teams.
I probably didn't think he'd play two games.
I imagined more, but it turned out that way and we thought it had to be done that way.
He wants to play, just like everyone else.”
Mickelson will tear Nick Fald's record for the highest number of Ryder Cup races.
With this, I could mark the end of Ryder's Cup career, which has never really matched the height of his personal record.
Mickelson has 18 victories, 20 defeats and seven ties, but Furyk said that his presence on the team also brings some intangible benefits.
“He’s funny, sarcastic, funny, he likes to make a fool out of people and it’s great to have him in a team space,” he explained.
“I think the younger players were having fun when they competed with him this week, which was fun to see.”
It gives more than just a game.”
European Captain Thomas Bjorn Knows He Calls A Big Advantage
Thomas Bjorn, the European captain, knows from experience that an important advantage at the beginning of the last day of games of individuals in the Ryder Cup can easily turn into an inconvenient ride.
The young Danish debuted in 1997 at a game in Valderrama, where the side, of which Seve Ballesteros was captain, had a five-point advantage over the Americans, and through the goal she made her way with an advantage only for the hair, with a score of 141⁄2-131⁄2.
“Don’t forget that we had a big advantage in Valderrama; we had a big advantage in Brookline, where we lost, and in Valderrama, where we won, but barely,” Bjorn (pictured) said after looking at the Class of 2018 team, who won with 5-3 on Friday and yesterday and led at the Le Golf National Highlight 10-6.
So the history of me and everyone in the team shows that the trophy is not over yet.
Play to the full tomorrow.
Go and do everything you need.
It's not over until it's written on the board.
We've got a goal to win this trophy, and we need to stay focused on it.
“I’ve been focusing on the 12 players on our side, but we’re also well aware of who’s standing on the opposite side – the best players in the world.”
Excited about the game of his players on a difficult court, Bjorn added: “I would never get ahead here.
It'll be a different day tomorrow.
Tomorrow, the game of individuals will be at the forefront, so this will have to be done differently.
It's nice to have a partner when things go well, but when you're there alone, your golfer's abilities are tested to the end.
It’s a message you need to convey to the players: Tomorrow you have to get the best out of yourself.
Now leave your partner behind and he has to get the best out.”
Contrary to Bjorn, Jim Furyk will encourage his players to play better than they played as a partner, except for Jordan Spieth and Justin Thomas, who have scored three points out of four.
Furyk himself was already on both sides of upheaval in the last day, as he was part of the winning team in Brookline before he ended up as a loser when Europe carried out “The Miracle in Medina”.
“I remember every damn word,” he replied when he was asked how Ben Crenshaw, captain of the year 1999, led his players to the last day of the game.
“Tomorrow we have 12 important matches, but we would like to start as fast as we saw in Brookline, as we have seen in Medina.
When this impetus gains perseverance, it puts a lot of pressure on the middle games.
We created a proper layout and set up the boys in a way we felt that tomorrow we were going to do something magical.”
Thomas was assigned the task of trying to lead the battle and clash with Rory McIlroy in the best game, and Paul Casey, Justin Rose, Jon Rahm, Tommy Fleetwood and Ian Poulter will be other Europeans in the upper half of the order.
“With this group of boys, I chose this order because I think it covers the entire schedule,” Bjorn said.
Germany's new warship postponed again
The newest frigate of the German Navy should have been handed over for use in 2014 in order to replace the aging Cold War warships, but due to the failure of the systems and the rapidly increasing costs, it will not continue until at least the next year, local media reported.
Surrenderection of the ship "Rheinland-Pfalz", the flagship of the new frigate class Baden-Wuerttemberg, is now postponed to the first half of 2019, says the newspaper Die Zeit, citing a military representative.
The vessel should join the Navy in 2014, but an ambitious project is plagued by worrying post-delivery issues.
Four Baden-Wuerttemberg ships ordered in 2007 will be replaced by an aging Bremen frigate class.
It is expected that they will have a powerful cannon, a series of anti-aircraft and anti-ship missiles, as well as some concealment technologies, such as reduced radar, infrared and acoustic signatures.
Other important features include longer maintenance spacings – the latest frigates could remain outside the domestic port for up to two years.
Continuous delays, however, mean that high-ranking warships, which are intended to enable Germany to influence the overseas, will become obsolete by the time of its use."
Unfortunate frigate F125 made its headline last year, when the German navy officially refused to pick up the ship and returned it to Blohm & Voss in Hamburg.
That was the first time the navy returned the ship to the shipyard after delivery.
Few are known about the reasons for the return, and the German media have identified a number of key "programme and technical shortcomings" that would make a warship useless if it had been deployed on a combat mission.
Weakinesses of the software were especially important, as Baden-Wuerttemberg-class vessels will be operated by a crew of about 120 sailors – only half of the workforce on older Bremen-class frigates.
It has also been shown that the ship is too heavy, which reduces its capacity and limits navy's chances for its future upgrade.
The 7000-tone ship "Rheinland-Pfalz" is said to be twice as heavier as ships of a similar class used by the Germans in World War II.
In addition to the lack of equipment, the price of the entire project – including crew training – has also become a problem.
It is expected to reach a dizzying €3.1 billion (US$3.6 billion) – from an initial €2.2 billion.
The problems with the latest frigates are becoming especially important in the light of the recent warnings that the naval power Germany is declining.
Earlier this year, Hans-Peter Bartels, Head of the Defence Committee of the German Parliament, admitted that the Navy was actually "lacking ships suitable for deployment."
An official said that the issue had been exacerbated over time because the old ships had been decommissioned and replacement vessels were not provided.
He regretted that the Navy could not be joined by any Baden-Wuerttemberg-class ship.
The National Fund eavesdrops the secret life of bats
A new study is being carried out at the estate in the Scottish Highlands, in which they strive to find out how bats use their diet in food hunting.
They hope that the findings will re-shedule the behavior of these unique flying mammals and help guide future conservation activities.
In the study, scientists of the National Fund for Scotland (NTS) will monitor small bats and tiny bats, as well as brown eaves of bats and riparian bats on the Inverewe Gardens estate in the Wester Ross area.
Special recorders will be set up in the key locations of the estate to monitor bat activities throughout the season.
NHS personnel and volunteers will also conduct mobile research using manual detectors.
The professional audio analysis of all the clips will confirm the frequency of bat calls and what types do they do.
Subsequently, they will draw up a habitat map and a report to create a detailed picture of their behavior throughout the landscape.
Rob Dewar, NTS Nature Conservation Advisor, hopes that the results will show which habitat areas are best suited for bats and how each individual species uses them.
This information will help determine the benefits of habitat management, such as the creation of meadows and the conservation of forests for bats and other related species.
The number of bat populations in Scotland and throughout the United Kingdom has declined sharply over the last century.
They are endangered by construction and developmental work, which affects shelters and habitat loss.
Wind turbines and lighting, as well as flies and some types of chemical treatment of building materials, as well as attacks by pet cats, are also at risk.
Bats aren't actually blind.
Due to nocturnal hunting habits, they help each other with their ears rather than with their eyes when catching prey.
To accurately determine errors and obstacles on the path of the year, they use a sophisticated technique of echolocation.
NTS, which is responsible for supplying more than 270 historic buildings, 38 important gardens and 76,000 hectares of land across the country, takes bats very seriously.
It employs ten specialists who regularly carry out research, examinations of shelters and periodic rescues.
The organization even established the first and so for now the only Scottish reserve specifically dedicated to bats in the Dumfries and Galloway.
David Thompson's manager says the estate is an ideal territory for them.
“At Threav, there’s an excellent area for bats,” he said.
“We have old buildings, lots of ancient trees, and all-quality habitat.
However, there is still a lot known about bats, so the work we do here and on other possessions will help us better understand what they need to thrive.”
He emphasized the importance of checking the presence of bats before carrying out maintenance work on properties, since the possible involuntary destruction of a single bin maternity hospital can lead to the death of up to 400 females and puppies, which can mean the collapse of the entire local population.
Bats are protected and it is forbidden to kill, harass or disturb them, and it is also forbidden to destroy their maternity hospitals.
Elisabeth Ferrell, Scottish leader of the Bat Conservation Fund, urged the public to cooperate.
“We still need to learn a lot about our bats, and we simply don’t know how well they stick to their population.”
Ronaldo Rejects Allegations of Rape and Lawyers Prepare for German Magazine Suggestion
Cristiano Ronaldo called the allegations of the rape he allegedly committed as "false news" and said that people wanted to “promote themselves” using his name.
His lawyers are preparing a lawsuit of the German magazine with the news Der Spiegel, who announced the allegations.
Portuguese and Juventus were accused of raping an American named Kathryn Mayorga in a hotel room in Las Vegas in 2009.
Der Spiegel reported on Friday that he had then paid $375,000 for silence about the event.
In announcementing 142 million followers in an Instagram Live video for several hours, Ronaldo, 33, called reports “fake news.”
“No, no, no, no, no.
“What they said is fake news,” a five-time Golden Ball Winner said.
“Using my name, they want to promote themselves.
- That's normal.
By mentioning my name, they want to become famous, which belongs to the work I'm doing.
“I’m a happy person and I wish you all the best,” the actor added.
According to Reuters, Ronaldo's lawyers are preparing to sue Der Spiegel for accusations they have dubbed "unacceptable reporting of suspicions about private lives".
Attorney Christian Schertz said the player would claim compensation for “non-pecuniary damage equivalent to the seriousness of the violation, which is probably one of the most severe violations of personality rights in recent years.”
An alleged incident is scheduled to take place in June 2009 at the Palms Hotel and Casino apartment in Las Vegas.
In the documentation filed at Clark County District Court in Nevada, he is expected to return to the player's room after a meeting at Mayorga's nightclub with Ronaldo, who allegedly raped her there.
Mayorga claims that Ronaldo fell to his knees after the event and told her that he was a “99 percent good man” who only disappoints in “one percent”.
The documents claim that Ronaldo confirmed sexual intercourse, but described it as consensual.
Mayorga also argues that she went to the police and that photographs of her injuries were taken at the hospital, and later arranged an extrajudicial settlement because she was “scaly afraid of retaliation” and was worried about “public humiliation.”
The 34-year-old says she wants to overrind the settlement because she continues to have severe trauma due to an alleged incident.
Ronaldo was on the verge of leaving Real Madrid to Manchester United at the time of the alleged attack, and this summer he moved to the Italian giant Juventus after a $100 million deal.
Brexit: UK ‘would deplores forever’ loss of car manufacturers
“Would be regretted forever” if Brexit had lost the status of the world’s leading car manufacturer, business secretary Greg Clark said.
He added that it was “worrisome” that Toyota UK had told the BBC that Britain’s withdrawal from the EU without a deal would temporarily halt production at Burnaston’s plant, near Derby.
“We need a deal,” said Mr. Clark.
Japan’s car manufacturer said the impact of delays at the border could lead to job loss in the event of non-deal Brexit.
Burnaston’s factory – which produces the Toyota Auris and Avensis models – produced nearly 150,000 cars last year, 90% of which were exported to the rest of the European Union.
"I believe that in the event that Britain leaves the European Union without a deal at the end of March, production in our factory would stop," said Marvin Cooke, Toyota's Managing Director at Burnaston.
Worries about how leaving the European Union without a deal will affect cross-border trade has also been expressed by other car manufacturers in the United Kingdom, including Honda, BMW and Jaguar Land Rover.
For example, BMW said it would close its Mini factory in Oxford for a month after Brexit.
The main concerns relate to what car manufacturers call supply chain-related risks in the case of Brexit.
Toyota’s production line operates according to the “right time” principle, with parts of suppliers from the UK and the EU for cars come every 37 minutes on request.
If the United Kingdom withdrew from the EU without a deal on 29 March, this could lead to border disturbances, which industry representatives say could lead to congestion and a shortage of parts.
Toyota would not be able to reconcile for more than a day at its Derbyshire plant for more than a day’s portions, the company’s representatives said, so production would stop.
G. Clark said Theresa May’s plan for future relations with the EU was “accurately calibrated to avoid such border checks”.
“We need to reach an agreement. We want to reach the best deal that will allow us, as I say, not only to enjoy the current success, but to seize this opportunity,” he told Today BBC Radio 4.
“Data not only by Toyota, but also of other manufacturers is that we must be absolutely able to continue a highly successful range of supply chains.”
Toyota was unable to comment on how long production would have been halted, but warned in the long term that additional costs would reduce the factory’s competitiveness and finally lead to job losses.
Peter Tsouvallaris, who has been working at Burnaston for 24 years and is the founder of the Unite union at the factory, said that his members are increasingly concerned: “In my experience, the lost job never returns.”
"We must present a precise and credible plan for our future relationship with the European Union."
Trump’s relationship with Rosenstein could have been rescheduled, says White House
Donald Trump’s important meeting with deputy Attorney General Rode Rosenstein could be “moved for another week” due to the outgoing struggle over Bretto Kavanaugh’s Supreme Court candidate, the White House said on Sunday.
Rosenstein oversees the work of Special Advisor Robert Mueller, who examines Russian pre-election interference, links between Trump’s co-workers and Russia, as well as a potential president’s obstruction of the judiciary.
The question whether Trump will dismiss the Attorney General's envoyant and jeopardize Mueller's independencence has powered Washington's rumors for months.
Earlier this month, the New York Times reported that Rosenstein was discussing the use of an eavesdropping device to record conversations with Trump and the possibility of removing the president on the basis of Amendment 25.
Rosenstein denied the report.
On the last Monday, he visited the White House in the midst of reporting that he plans to resignate.
Instead, the meeting with Trump, which was then at the United Nations Conference in New York, was proclaimed Thursday.
Trump said Rosenstein “would rather wouldn’t forgive,” and the meeting was then postponed in order to evade the conflict by interrogating the Senate’s Judiciary, in which Kavanaugh and one of the women accused of inadequate sexual behavior, Dr. Fordova, testified.
On Friday, Trump ordered a one-day investigation into charges against Kavanaugh, which further postponed Senate votes.
Trump’s spokeswoman, Sarah Sanders, appeared on Fox News on Sunday.
When she was asked to meet Rosenstein, she said: “The date has not yet been set for him. It could have happened this week, and given everything else that takes place in the Supreme Court, he could be postponed for another week.
We’ll see. I always like to make sure you’re informed.”
Some reporters would have objected to this. Sanders did not convene a White House meeting with reporters since September 10.
Chris Wallace's host asked her why.
Sanders said the rarity of the meetings should not be attributable to reluctating to “setting up” TV-reporters, but she said: “She couldn’t deny that they’re standing up.”
She then mentioned that the direct contact between Trump and the press would increase.
“The president conducts more press conferences than anyone in front of him,” she said, adding: “We’ve looked at the numbers.”
“However, if reporters can contact the President of the United States directly with their question, this is infinitely better than talking to me.
We’re trying to do this as much as possible and you’ve seen that we’ve done it often over the last few weeks and that you’re going to have a conference where you’ll be able to talk to the President of the United States.”
Trump regularly accepts questions when he leaves the White House or attends open meetings or press conferences with away statesmen.
Independent press conferences are rare.
In New York this week, the president may have shown why, when he appeared freely and partially bizarrely in front of the assembled journalists.
Letter from the Minister of Health to EU workers at NHS Scotland on Brexit-Related Disagreements
The Minister of Health wrote to the EU staff working at the Scottish NHS to express the country's gratitude and the desire to continue their work even after Brexit.
Jeane Freeman, SMEs, sent the letter less than six months before the withdrawal of the United Kingdom from the European Union.
The Scottish Government has already pledged to pay the costs of applications to regulate the status of EU citizens working in its decentralised public services.
“During the summer, negotiations between the United Kingdom and the European Union for the Withdrawal continued, approaching the adoption of decisions this autumn.
However, the United Kingdom government has also accelerated preparations for a possible no-deal scenario.
I know it has to be a great time for all of you.
Therefore, I would like to emphasise once again how much I appreciate the contribution of each staff member, irrespective of his nationality.
Colleagues from all over the European Union and non-member countries bring valuable experience and skills that strengthen and improve the work of the health sector and benefit patients and the communities we care for.
In Scotland, you can feel completely at home. We very much want to stay here.”
Christion Abercrombie Urgently Surgerized After Head Injury
A liner supporter of the Tennessee State Tigers team, Christion Abercrombie, underwent an emergency operation following a head injury in 31:27 against Vanderbilt Commodores on Saturday's defeat.
Tennessee University's head coach told reporters that the injury had occurred just before halftime.
