Foreign buyers will be charged a higher fee when buying a property in the UK
Foreign buyers will be charged a higher fee when buying real estate in the UK, and additional money will be used to help the homelesses according to the new plans of Tories
Move will neutralize Corbyn's efforts to attract young voters
An increased humage will be charged those who do not pay tax in the United Kingdom
The Treasury expects to raise up to £120 million a year, and it'll donate them to help the homeless.
Foreign buyers will be charged a higher level of hipcupine when buying real estate in the UK, and the accumulated money will be allocated to the homeless, Theresa May said today.
The move should be understood as an attempt to neutralize Jeremy Corbyn’s efforts to attract young voters with promises to provide more affordable housing and increased taxation of high-income people.
Elevated humage will be billed to persons and companies that do not pay tax in the United Kingdom, and additional money will stimulate the government's efforts to eliminate homelessness.
A surcharge – which will be charged in addition to the current stamp duty, including elevated levels introduced two years ago for other homes and rental properties – could amount to no less than three percent.
The Treasury expects it to raise up to GBP 120 million per year through the imposition of the measure.
It is estimated that non-citizens of the United Kingdom bought 13% of newly built London real estate, resulting in an increase in prices and a heavier purchase of the first property.
Many of the country’s rich areas – especially in the capital – have become “Ghost Cities” as the number of buyers who spend most of their time outside the country increases.
A new politician was adopted just a few weeks after Boris Johnson called for reducing hipculaw to make it easier for young people to buy their first home.
He accused large-scale construction companies of raising prices by raking land that they did not actually use, but he urged Ms May to abandon quotas for affordable housing in order to correct Britain's "residential embarrassment."
Mr. Corbyn has announced an attractive series of proposed housing reforms, including the control of rents and the completion of forcible evictions without errors.
In addition, he wants to give city councils greater powers to build new homes.
“Last year I said, “Last year I said I would dedicate my primordial leadership to the renewal of Britain’s dreams – that life needs to be better for every new generation.
It is also the correction of our non-functional housing market.
Great Britain will always be open to people who want to live, work and create a life here.
However, it cannot be right for people who do not live in the United Kingdom and for foreign companies to buy homes as easy as for guarant British citizens.
For too many people, the dream of one’s own home is all too far away, and humiliating homelessness remains too real.”
Jack Ross: “My ultimate goal is to manage the Scotland Club”
Sunderland boss, Jack Ross, says his “final goal” one day is to become the manager of Scotland.
Scotch, 42, enjoys the challenge of reviving North-East, which currently ranks third place in the Premier League, with three points less than first-placed.
