I’d only have had this opportunity if I’d been successful in running the club.”
Rosso's predecessors at the helm of Sunderland were Dick Advocaat, David Moyes, Sam Allardyce, Martin O'Neill, Roy Keane, Gus Poyet and Paulo Di Canio.
Former Alloa Athletic, who had previously declined offers from Barnsley and Ipswich Town, says that, as the successor to such great names, he did not feel fear.
“Success is measured for me with the question ‘Can I take this club back to the Premier League?’”
Given the structure and capabilities, this club undoubtedly belongs to the Premier League," he said.
"It's not an easy way to get there, but I'd probably only consider myself successful if the club could go there again."
Ross has only worked as a manager for three years after he was an assistant leader in Dumbarton and has been part of the Hearts Coaching Staff for 15 months.
Alloi then helped to prevent a third-leaf loss, and St Mirren led to next season’s championship title.
Ross says he now feels more comfortable than ever playing for Clyde, Hartlepool, Falkirk, St Mirren and Hamilton Academical.
“It was probably a real turning point,” he recalled Alloe’s takeover.
"I was really convinced that management was the right thing for me, much more so than acting.
It sounds ridiculous because I was doing well as a player, I made a relatively good living with it and I also achieved some greater successes.
But the game can become difficult.
We have to deal with many things every week.
In terms of stress and pressure at work, I still cope with it, but management simply feels like the right thing for myself.
“I’ve always wanted to run a club and I’m doing it right now. I feel most comfortable in my entire adult life.”
You can listen to the full interview on Sunday, September 30, on Sportsound at Radio Scotland between 12 and 13:00 (BST).
The result of the research: the ideal time for beer is on Saturday at 17.30
Summer heat has increased the revenues of hard-to-break British pubs, but pressure on restaurant chains has also increased.
Pub and bar groups increased sales by 2.7% in July, but restaurant revenues fell by 4.8 percent.
Peter Martin, CGA’s CGA business consultant, said: “Perseverant sunny weather and England’s participation in the World Cup, which lasted longer than expected, meant that July followed a similar pattern as June in the past month, when pub revenue increased by 2.8 percent, except that restaurants were affected even harder.
The decline in restaurant traffic by 1.8% in June increased in July.
Traffic in pubs and bars with drinks increased in comparison with each other, as turnover in restaurants declined.
Due to the sunny weather, sales in pubs, which mainly offer food, but not as dramatically as with restaurant managers, also decreased.
It seems that people simply wanted to go for a drink.
In various pubs and bars, beverage sales increased by 6.6 percent in one month, and food sales fell by 3%.”
Paul Newman of the Group of Leisure Analysts and RSM Hospitality said: “These results continue the trend that we have been observing since the end of April.
When it comes to the catering market, the biggest factors remain the weather and the impact of large social or sporting events.
It is not surprising that restaurant groups continue to fight, although a fall of 4.8 percent on a year-on-year-old basis will be especially painful due to constant cost pressures.
It wouldn’t be possible for owners of food bars for a long horty summer at the right time, and it’s only time to say whether the necessary recovery will bring the more moderate temperatures we experienced in August.”
Total sales in pubs and restaurants, including new openings, were 2.7% in July, suggesting a slowing brand introduction.
Coffer Peach Tracker, a benchmark for sales in the pub, bars and restaurants sector, collects and analyzes data on the performance of 47 business groups with a total turnover of more than £9 billion and is an established industrial reference.
