Hundreds of Migrant Children Quietly Migrated to Tent Camp at Texas Border
The number of detained migrant children rose sharply, even if the number of monthly border crossings remained relatively unchanged, partly because the gross rhetoric and policies imposed by the Trump administration made it more difficult for children to be handed over to sponsors.
Traditionally, most undocumented migrant sponsors are afraid that applying for a child would jeopardize their own chances of staying in the country.
Risks increased in June, when federal authorities announced that potential sponsors and other household family members would have to submit their fingerprints and that data will be shared with immigration authorities.
Last week, Matthew Albence, a high-ranked official at the Immigration and Tariff Agency, testified before Congress that the agency had arrested dozens of people who had asked to sponsor unaccompanied minors.
The agency later confirmed that 70% of those arrested did not have prior criminal records.
“Nearly 80 percent of people who are either sponsors or household members of sponsors are illegal in the country, and a large part of them are penalized aliens.
That’s why we’re continuing the search for these individuals,” said Albence.
In order to treat children more quickly, officials have introduced new rules that will require some of them to appear in court within one month of being detained, and not after 60 days, which was the previous standard, workers at the shelter said.
Many will appear with a videoconferencing call, and not personally to defend themselves before an immigration referee.
Those who are considered unwelcomable will be expelled quickly.
The longer children in custody, the more likely they are to become anxious or depressive, which, according to workers in shelters and reports that have arisen in recent months, can lead to violent outbursts or attempts to escape.
Defenders said these concerns were greater in larger institutions, e.g. Tornill, where signs of a child’s distress are more likely to go unnoticed due to their size.
They added that relocating children to the city of tents, without emotionally preparing them or giving them the opportunity to say goodbye to friends, can complicate the trauma that many of them face.
