If we fail in these tests, there is a long-winter democracy.
But I'm sure we won't fail because of the lesson I learned in Prague.
My mother was a Czechoslovakian Judish, deported to Auschwitz by the same Nazi regime that once occupied my embassy home.
She survived, immigrated to America, and told me 60 years later to ignite scabbage candles on a table with swastiks.
How can’t you be optimistic about our future with such an inheritance?”
Norman Eisen, a regular member of Brookings Institution, is President of Citizens for Responsibility and Ethics in Washington, D.C. and author of "The Last Palace: Europe's Turbulent Century in Five Lives and One Legendary House."
