Earthquakes and Tsunamies in Indonesia
At least 384 people died in a severe earthquake and tsunamium that hit the Indonesian city of Palu on Friday, officials said, and the number of casualties is expected to increase.
In the case of interrupted communication connections, rescuers from Donggalo's regency, an area north of Palu that is closer to the epicenter of an earthquake of magnitude 7.5, could not get any information.
More than 16,000 people were evacuated in Palu after the earthquake.
Some key information about Palu and Donggali in Sulavezi:
Palu is the capital of Central Sulavezi, a landscape located at the end of a narrow bay on the western coast of Sulavezi Island and is estimated to have an estimated 379,9,800 inhabitants in 2017.
The city celebrated its 40th anniversary when it was hit by an earthquake and a tsunami.
Donggala is a landscape that stretches along more than 300 km (180 miles) on the northwest of Sulavezi Island.
Regency, an administrative unit under the province, has an estimated 299,000 inhabitants in 2017.
The main economic activities of the Central Sulavezi region are aquaculture and farming, especially in the Donggala central region.
Nickel mining is also an important activity in the region, which mostly takes place in Morovali, on the opposite side of Sulavesius.
Palu and Donggalo have been repeatedly affected by a tsunami in the last 100 years, according to the Indonesian Damping Agency.
In 1938, the tsunami caused the deaths of more than 200 people and destroyed hundreds of houses in Donggala.
In 1996, the tsunami also affected the northern Donggala and caused the deaths of nine people.
Indonesia sits on a seismically activating Tychooceanic Ring, so earthquakes occur here regularly.
In recent years, some major earthquakes and tsunamies have been as follows:
2004: A major earthquake on the west coast of the Indonesian province of Aceh in northern Sumatri on 26 December caused a tsunami that affected 14 countries, killing 226,000 people along the coast of the Indian estimate, of which more than half in Aceh.
2005: A series of powerful earthquakes hit western Sumatra in late March and early April.
Hundreds have died on Nias Island along the Sumatra coast.
2006: Earthquake of magnitude 6.8 struck the southern part of Java, Indonesia’s most populated island, triggering a tsunami that flooded its southern coastage causing the deaths of nearly 700 people.
2009: Earthquake of magnitude 7.6 occurred near Padang, the capital of West Sumatra Province.
More than 1100 people have died.
2010: Earthquake of magnitude 7.5 hit one of the Mentawai islands and triggered a tsunami of up to 10 meters in height, destroying dozens of villages and causing the deaths of about 300 people.
2016: Shallow earthquake struck the Pidie Jaya regency in Aceh and caused death and panic when people remembered a deadly earthquake and tsunami in 2004.
This time the tsunami did not go off, but the fallen buildings caused the deaths of more than 100 people.
20180: Major earthquakes affected Indonesia’s Lombok tourist island and resulted in the deaths of more than 500 people, mostly on the northern side of the island.
The earthquake destroyed thousands of buildings and temporarily detained thousands of tourists.
