Federal Prosecutors Claim Rare Death Penalty for Suspensor of Terrorist Attack in New York City
Federal prosecutors in New Citry are seeking the death penalty for Sajfull Saipov, a suspect in a terrorist attack in New York, in which eight people were killed. It is a rare sentence that has not been carried out for a federal crime in the country since 1953.
Saipov, 30, is expected to use a rented delivery truck to carry out an attack on a cycling route along the West Side Highway in Lower Manhattan, when he ran over several pedestrians and a cyclist in October.
In order to justify the death penalty, prosecutors will have to prove that Saipov “intentionally” killed eight victims and “deliberately” caused serious bodily injuries, according to a notification of a request for the death penalty filed in New York’s Southern District.
According to the court document, the reason for the death penalty can be any of the accusations.
Several weeks after the attack, Saipov’s grand federal jury informed Saip about an indictment in 22 points, which included eight charges of murder to help extortion, which are usually used by federal prosecutors in organized crime cases, as well as an accusation for the violence and destruction of motor vehicles.
The attack demanded “significant planning and intent,” prosecutors said, describing Saip’s way as “abominant, cruel and depraved.”
"Sayfullo Habibullaevic Saipov caused injury, damage and loss to the families and friends of Diego Enrique Angelini, Nicholas Cleves, Ann-Laure Decadt, Darrena Draka, Ariel Erli, Hernan Ferruchi, Hernan Diego Mendoza and Alejandro Damian Pagnucco," it said.
Five of the victims were tourists from Argentina.
The District Court of South County imposed its last death sentence ten years ago.
