China, the world's second-largest economy, has pledged to make an eye-eye with the U.S. and is unlikely to blink soon.
In order for Washington to subside, China would have to agree to his demands, but the U.S. publicity for Chinese President Xi Jinping would be too embarrassing.
However, experts say that if Beijing knows how to make the right moves, U.S. pressure in a trade war could have a positive impact on China in the long term by reducing the interdependence of both economies.
“The fact that a rapid political decision in Washington or Beijing could create conditions that would trigger an economic turmoil in any of the countries is, in fact, much more dangerous than the observers thought so far,” said Abigail Grace, an expellorator focusing on Asia on the Brain Trust Center for New American Security.
Syria 'ready' for the return of refugees, the foreign minister says
Syria says it is ready for the voluntary return of refugees and is asking for help to rebuild a country devastated by a war of more than seven years.
In an address to the United Nations General Assembly, Foreign Minister Valid al-Moualem said the situation in the country was improving.
"Today the situation on the ground is more stable and safer in the fight against terrorism," he said.
The government continues to rehabilitate areas destroyed by terrorists in order to rebuild the normal situation.
All conditions are now met to ensure that refugees who have been forced to leave the country voluntarily due to terrorism and one-sided economic measures aimed at their daily lives and subsistences are met.
The United Nations estimates that more than 5.5 million Syrians have escaped the country since the beginning of the war in 2011.
Other six millions of people still living in the country are in need of humanitarian assistance.
Al-Moualem said the Syrian regime would welcome assistance in restoring a devastated country.
However, he pointed out that he would not accept conditional aid or assistance from countries that sponsored rebels.
Europe wins in the Ryder Cup in Paris
By winning against the U.S. team with a final score of 16.5 to 10.5 at the Le Golf National at Paris in France, the European team won the Ryder Cup for 2018.
The U.S. has now lost six times in a row on European soil and hasn't won the Ryder Cup since 1993.
Europe once again scored the crown when Danish captain Thomas Bjorn’s team scored 14.5 points needed to win over the United States.
American star Phil Mickelson, who had problems through most of the championship, at the 16th hole in a par-3 game, swiped the ball into the water, which forced him to hand the game to Francesco Molinari.
Italian golfer Molinari shone through all laps and became one of the four players who at any time since the start of the championship in the current form in 1979 achieved a score of 5-0-0.
Jordan Spieth defeated the lowest-ranked European team player Thorbjorn Olesen of Denmark with 5 and 4.
Dustin Johnson lost 2 to 1 to Ian Poulter of England, who may have played in the Ryder Cup for the last time.
Veteran of the eight Ryder Cups, the Spaniard Sergio Garcia, with 25.5 points, became Europe's winnest player in the championship of all time.
“I don’t usually cry, but I can’t help myself today.
This year was hard.
I'm very grateful to Thomas for lifting me up and he believed in me.
I'm so happy, I'm so lucky we won the trophy again.
It's about the team, and I'm really glad that I was able to help," Garcia said emotionally after the European victory.
He handed over the relay to his compatriot John Ram, who defeated Tiger Woods on Sunday with 2 and 1 in individual games.
“I feel incredibly proud that I managed to defeat Tiger Woods. When I was growing up, I watched his games,” said 23-year-old Rahm.
Woods has lost all four games he played in France, and he now holds a career record in the Ryder Cup 13-21-3.
It’s an unusual statistic for one of the greatest players of all time, having won 14 big titles after Jack Nicklaus.
The U.S. team struggled all week to find the right way, except for Patrick Reed, Justin Thomas and Tony Finau, who played high-level golf throughout the championship.
The American captain said after a bad performance for his team: “I’m so proud of these guys, they fought.”
In the morning, we put some pressure on Europe for a moment.
We've failed.
Hat down in front of Thomas.
He's an excellent captain.
All of his 12 players played very well.
We're gonna have to get together again, I'm going to work with the American PGA and our Ryder Cup Committee, and we're gonna move forward.
“I really love these 12 guys, and I’m proud to be their captain.
- Hats off.
“They played us.”
Red Tide News: Concentrations along the coasts of Pinellas, Manatee and Saratos are declining
The most recent report of the Florida Commission for the Protection of Fish and Game (FWC) shows a general decrease in red tide concentrations in parts of the Tampa Bay area.
The FWC Commission reported more diffused flowering in Pinellas, Manatee, Saratos, Charlotte and Collier, indicating a decrease in concentrations.
Red tide stretches about 130 miles from Pinellas County to the southern counties of Lee.
Zaplate is seen about 10 miles off the shores of Hillsborough district, but in fewer places than last week.
Red tide was also observed in Pasco County.
Last week there have been reports of medium concentrations in the Pinellas district and along its coast, low to high concentrations off the coast of Hillsborough County, basic to high concentrations in the Manatee district, basic to high concentrations in the Saratosa district, and along its coast, basic to medium concentrations in the Charlotte district, basic to high concentrations in the Lee County county and along its coast, as well as low concentrations in the Collier district.
Respiratory irritation continues to be reported from Pinellas, Manatee, Sarasota, Lee and Collier.
Respiratory irritation was not reported from Northwestern Florida in the past week.
